at $x=-1$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba472f7-f313-51f2-a104-7a19c58b2c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281365} {:answer/id #uuid "edef8f5b-d98f-5c89-bd5d-61900e8465e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281366} {:answer/id #uuid "dd36ed9f-899d-58cb-a7ac-30d94879b825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281367} {:answer/id #uuid "400cf076-e23e-50f7-b419-a4e527afd22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281368} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c86c03-e515-520c-b8c6-5a893d8f89a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edef8f5b-d98f-5c89-bd5d-61900e8465e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281366} :answer-field/id #uuid "046fffba-77af-5ed3-9357-e6ff4dd88b10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ece8891-3519-531e-9799-b3b357c6d488" :question/math-academy-id "q-104049" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980965/assets/5143df65d0f99c6511dc8ab13262831669c88535e19c986dacdb364e04847f58.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee67c1dc-eacb-5290-976a-9700b0eb5b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281372} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e377c8-9b79-599b-8612-9cc262926f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281373} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f16478-5d69-5133-a16a-75fd398cd09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281374} {:answer/id #uuid "b77bc2cd-37c7-5683-bab4-f899295546ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281375} {:answer/id #uuid "9bff73b9-ed07-55c0-a80c-b739a7e982b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e377c8-9b79-599b-8612-9cc262926f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281373} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e8d9b6-add4-54f0-81e2-d7200843e3da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df1e4f10-7dbe-5216-a5eb-493a05d98b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980965/assets/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c504b73-3714-5793-9249-3061d1a80938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281379} {:answer/id #uuid "28e5f4e2-ee74-578d-8b9f-93f1efb71068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281380} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b90e36-78c9-5372-b78d-bbacebb2dcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281381} {:answer/id #uuid "9e46beea-3bba-5e34-99fc-b1ec80f604e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281382} {:answer/id #uuid "08a3fc23-0567-503a-baf2-f78b303817af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e46beea-3bba-5e34-99fc-b1ec80f604e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281382} :answer-field/id #uuid "505c801a-8430-5cf0-b998-0ff275882467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9378234-bce1-51db-833b-fbd7d05969fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-104052" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980965/assets/8086893e4587e68f8257b75f2070cf622fa141199d6bd8960f7c4fd8e7bcb349.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}]}
